dementia effects 850,000 people in the  UK but it's not a disease in itself it's  the name given to a range of symptoms  that affect the brain including memory  loss and difficulties worth thinking  problem-solving or language it has many  different causes but they all result in  the death of brain cells and symptoms  that get worse with time Alzheimer's  disease is the most common form of  dementia with 550,000 people affected in  the UK it's caused by a buildup of  abnormal proteins in the brain the  results in the loss of connections  between nerve cells and ultimately the  death of brain tissue the number of  people in the UK with dementia is  expected to reach 1 million by 2025 and  2 million by 2050 that's largely because  of the aging population dementia  generally affects older people lifestyle  particularly smoking and diet can also  increase the chances of some forms of  dementia it's only recently that the  full impact of dementia on society has  been recognized pushing it up the  political agenda around six hundred and  seventy thousand people currently care  for someone with dementia many will have  given up work to do so and the cost of  dementia to the economy has been put at  26 billion pounds a year the leaders of  the g8 nations have come together to  make dementia a priority with the aim of  finding a medicine to slow or halt the  symptoms by 2025 because it's a  condition of older age just delaying the  symptoms by five years would halve the  number of cases now a drug called solo  nezam AB has been shown to slow the  decline in brain function by 30% if it's  given in the early stages and with a  sharp increase in research funding there  is a feeling that it's only a matter of  time before doctors do have effective  treatment
 

